====================================================================
DOMAIN: Naturalism, Darwinism, and the Evolutionary Reading of Marxism
SEARCH_DATE: 2026-06-10
PAPERS_FOUND: 13 total (High: 6, Medium: 5, Low: 2)
SEARCH_SOURCES: SEP, IEP, PhilPapers, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex, CORE
====================================================================

DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
This domain covers Kautsky's naturalistic and evolutionary framing of
historical materialism, his engagement with Darwin and Haeckel, and the
broader question of how socialist thinkers at the Second International
integrated Darwinian biology with Marxist social theory. The key debates
concern whether Kautsky's evolutionary Marxism constitutes a form of
"social Darwinism" -- appropriating biological selection logic for social
analysis -- or whether it maintained a principled distinction between natural
and social evolution. Weikart's foundational study (1999) argues that German
socialists including Kautsky produced a distinctive "socialist Darwinism"
that selectively borrowed from Darwin while repudiating Malthusian and
competitive-selection applications. Blackledge's critique challenges this
framing as caricature, arguing that Kautsky's early evolutionary framework
was more sophisticated and dialectically aware than the "evolutionist"
label suggests.

A second set of debates concerns the Haeckelian monist tradition: Kautsky
was influenced by Ernst Haeckel's "monism" -- the view that all natural and
social processes obey unified evolutionary laws -- which shaped his ethics
(Ethics and the Materialist Conception of History, 1906) and his
Vermehrung und Entwicklung (1910). The Weir and Stewart/Hossfeld/Levit
papers illuminate how Haeckelian monism operated as a bridge between
naturalistic Darwinism and socialist ethics. Recent scholarship (Gronow
2016, Rowley 2024) situates Kautsky's naturalism within the formation of
Second International Marxism and its contested relation to orthodox
scientific categories.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
Kautsky's philosophy of history cannot be understood without grasping his
naturalistic commitments -- the claim that social development follows laws
analogous to (though not identical with) natural selection. This domain
provides the conceptual vocabulary and scholarly debate for assessing whether
Kautsky's determinism is "biologistic" or genuinely materialist, and how his
position compares to contemporaries like Plekhanov, Bogdanov, and Spencer.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
Scholarship specifically on Kautsky's Vermehrung und Entwicklung (1910) as
a philosophical text remains thin in English-language secondary literature.
The connection between Kautsky's Darwinism and his ethics (the 1906 work)
has received less sustained attention than his political economy. Entries
marked INCOMPLETE lack publicly available abstracts.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
Cross-reference with Domain 1 (general Kautsky scholarship) to situate the
evolutionary dimension within Kautsky's overall philosophy of history; the
distinction between natural and social evolution is central to assessing his
determinism and his theory of progress.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Socialist Darwinism thesis: [4 papers] - Weikart, Stack, and others argue
  German socialists produced a distinctive synthesis of Darwin and Marx that
  selectively biologized social theory.
- Critique of reductionism: [3 papers] - Blackledge, Gronow argue the
  "socialist Darwinism" label misrepresents the actual dialectical complexity
  of Kautsky's evolutionary framework.
- Haeckelian monism tradition: [3 papers] - Weir, Stewart/Hossfeld/Levit
  examine how monist naturalism shaped socialist ethics in Germany.
- Historical materialism and natural selection: [2 papers] - Lattansi-Silveus,
  Rowley examine whether Darwinian selection logic can be coherently applied
  to social evolution.
====================================================================
}

@book{weikart1999socialist,
  author = {Weikart, Richard},
  title = {Socialist Darwinism: Evolution in German Socialist Thought from Marx to Bernstein},
  year = {1999},
  publisher = {International Scholars Publications},
  address = {San Francisco and London},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Weikart argues that major German socialist theorists from Marx
  to Bernstein -- including Kautsky, Bebel, and Lange -- developed a distinctive
  "socialist Darwinism" that integrated Darwinian evolutionary concepts into
  socialist theory. Rather than simply rejecting or uncritically adopting
  Darwinism, these thinkers selectively appropriated evolutionary ideas while
  rejecting Malthusian competition, producing a politically oriented naturalism
  that grounded socialist hopes in biological and social progress. Kautsky
  receives extended treatment as a central figure who sought to demonstrate the
  natural inevitability of socialist development.

  RELEVANCE: This is the foundational study for assessing the charge that
  Kautsky's Marxism is a form of social Darwinism. Weikart's analysis of
  Kautsky's Vermehrung und Entwicklung and Ethics and the Materialist
  Conception of History provides the primary scholarly framework for
  understanding how Kautsky integrated biological evolutionary concepts with
  historical materialism. Essential for any account of Kautsky's naturalism.

  POSITION: Argues for a distinctive "socialist Darwinist" tradition in German
  social democracy that biologizes historical progress while maintaining
  socialist political conclusions.
  },
  keywords = {Kautsky, Darwinism, socialist-Darwinism, evolution, German-socialism, Second-International, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{weikart1993origins,
  author = {Weikart, Richard},
  title = {The Origins of Social Darwinism in Germany, 1859-1895},
  journal = {Journal of the History of Ideas},
  year = {1993},
  volume = {54},
  number = {3},
  pages = {469--488},
  doi = {10.2307/2710024},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Weikart traces the emergence of social Darwinism in Germany
  from Darwin's reception in the 1860s through the 1890s, distinguishing
  between laissez-faire social Darwinism (appropriating Malthusian struggle)
  and socialist Darwinism (appropriating progressive evolution). The article
  shows that "social Darwinism" in Germany was not a unified ideology but
  a contested and politically diverse field, with socialist thinkers deploying
  Darwin against both capitalism and competing biologistic conservatisms.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the historical and conceptual background for locating
  Kautsky's evolutionary Marxism within the broader German reception of
  Darwinism. Distinguishes the socialist use of Darwin from the more
  familiar right-wing varieties of social Darwinism, which is essential for
  assessing whether Kautsky's naturalism is properly called "social Darwinist."

  POSITION: Identifies social Darwinism as a contested and politically
  ambivalent phenomenon in Germany, with distinct socialist and conservative
  variants.
  },
  keywords = {social-Darwinism, Germany, Darwin-reception, evolution, socialist-Darwinism, High}
}

@article{blackledge2004weikart,
  author = {Blackledge, Paul},
  title = {On Richard Weikart's Socialist Darwinism: Evolution in German Socialist Thought from Marx to Bernstein},
  journal = {Historical Materialism},
  year = {2004},
  volume = {12},
  number = {1},
  pages = {213--232},
  doi = {10.1163/156920604773564050},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Blackledge's critical review argues that Weikart's framing
  of German socialist evolutionary thought as "socialist Darwinism" caricatures
  the actual dialectical sophistication of thinkers like Kautsky. Blackledge
  contends that Kautsky's early engagement with Darwin was embedded in a
  broader materialist framework that did not simply biologize social theory but
  sought to articulate the specific conditions of human historical development.
  The review identifies conceptual conflations and anachronistic readings in
  Weikart's account.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the major scholarly counterposition to Weikart's thesis
  and is essential for understanding the debate over whether Kautsky's
  evolutionary naturalism constitutes a form of social Darwinism. Blackledge's
  defense of Kautsky's early work as containing genuine dialectical insights
  is central to the revisionist rehabilitation of Kautsky in recent Marxist
  historiography.

  POSITION: Defends Kautsky's early evolutionary materialism against the
  "social Darwinist" label, arguing for a more dialectically complex reading.
  },
  keywords = {Kautsky, Weikart, socialist-Darwinism, historical-materialism, critique, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{blackledge2006kautsky,
  author = {Blackledge, Paul},
  title = {Karl Kautsky and Marxist Historiography},
  journal = {Science \& Society: A Journal of Marxist Thought and Analysis},
  year = {2006},
  volume = {70},
  number = {3},
  pages = {337--359},
  doi = {10.1521/siso.70.3.337},
  abstract = {The traditional dismissive characterization of Karl Kautsky's Marxism as a form of mechanical or even Darwinian evolutionism is a caricature that obscures the very real and important contribution he made to historical materialism in the early years of the last century. Kautsky's historical studies of both the early Church and Reformation "communism," as well as his attempts to analyze the contemporary Russian and American social formations, show that, while Kautsky's Marxism suffered from consistent political weaknesses, his theory of history, especially as it was articulated in the years before 1910, was sophisticated and powerful. While the power of Kautsky's Marxism became muted after this period, the weaknesses of his later works should not be allowed to obscure the insights contained within his earlier work; insights that informed some of the best work of the next generation of Marxists.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Blackledge argues that the standard dismissal of Kautsky as
  a mechanical evolutionist distorts his actual contribution to historical
  materialism. Drawing on Kautsky's early historical writings, Blackledge shows
  that Kautsky's evolutionism was never simply a transposition of Darwinian
  biology to social history but incorporated key insights about class struggle,
  political agency, and the specificity of human history. The weaknesses of
  Kautsky's later work should not retrospectively color his earlier, more
  sophisticated contributions.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses the relationship between Kautsky's naturalistic
  framework and his historiographical method, showing how evolutionary
  categories shaped but did not determine his account of historical
  development. Central for evaluating the claim that Kautsky's philosophy of
  history is reducible to biologistic determinism.

  POSITION: Revisionist defense of Kautsky's historical materialism as
  containing genuine analytical depth despite its evolutionary naturalist
  framework.
  },
  keywords = {Kautsky, historical-materialism, historiography, evolution, Second-International, High}
}

@book{gronow2016formation,
  author = {Gronow, Jukka},
  title = {On the Formation of Marxism: Karl Kautsky's Theory of Capitalism, the Marxism of the Second International and Karl Marx's Critique of Political Economy},
  year = {2016},
  publisher = {Brill},
  doi = {10.1163/9789004306653},
  abstract = {Preface and Acknowledgements Introduction PART I: KAUTSKY'S MARXISM 1. Organised Capitalism, the General Cartel and the Proletariat 2. The Dispute over Revisionism 3. The Theory of Immiseration, Socialist Consciousness and the Intellectuals 4. Socialism as Science 5. The Capitalist Law of Appropriation: Kautsky's Interpretation of Karl Marx's Economic Thought 6. The Centralisation of Capital and Monopoly Formation 7. Imperialism and the Relation between Industrial and Agrarian Countries 8. Imperialism and Its Alternatives 9. Imperialism as the Last Stage of Capitalism 10. Theoretical Sources of Kautsky's and Lenin's Studies on Imperialism 11. Imperialism as the Truth about Capitalism 12. Parliamentary Democracy and Revolutionary Tactics 13. The Question of Democracy and Dictatorship: Lenin's Critique of Kautsky the Renegade PART II: MARX'S MARXISM 14. The Immanent Critique and the Natural Rights Theory 15. John Locke, Adam Smith and Karl Marx's Critique of Private Property 16. The Principle of Labour 17. The Theory of Increasing Misery and the Critique of Capitalism Conclusion Bibliography Index},
  abstract_source = {s2},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Gronow analyzes how Kautsky's theory of capitalism and
  historical development was formed at the intersection of Marx's critique of
  political economy and the naturalistic evolutionary paradigm of the late
  nineteenth century. The book argues that Kautsky's synthesis of Darwinian
  naturalism with Marxist political economy produced a distinctive
  "scientific Marxism" that shaped the entire tradition of the Second
  International. Gronow pays particular attention to how Kautsky understood
  the laws of capitalist development as analogous to natural laws, giving
  historical materialism its deterministic character.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the most thorough recent treatment of how Kautsky's
  naturalistic commitments were integrated into his overall theoretical
  framework. Directly relevant to understanding the philosophical underpinnings
  of Kautsky's philosophy of history, including his claims about the
  scientific necessity of socialism and the role of evolutionary naturalism.

  POSITION: Situates Kautsky within the formation of orthodox Marxism as a
  naturalistic-scientific enterprise that shaped all subsequent Second
  International Marxism.
  },
  keywords = {Kautsky, formation-of-Marxism, Second-International, capitalism, political-economy, evolution, High}
}

@article{nygaard2009constructing,
  author = {Nygaard, Bertel},
  title = {Constructing Marxism: Karl Kautsky and the French Revolution},
  journal = {History of European Ideas},
  year = {2009},
  volume = {35},
  number = {4},
  pages = {450--464},
  doi = {10.1016/j.histeuroideas.2009.04.002},
  abstract = {Karl Kautsky's writings on the French Revolution were crucial to the construction not only of the Marxist interpretation of the Revolution, which was perhaps the most important reference point for the historiography of that event during the 20th century, but even of Marxism itself as a comprehensive, systematic theory partly based on historical studies. However, these writings have been neglected and practically forgotten for decades, mainly because of the general rejection of Kautsky's theories after the October Revolution of 1917, in Marxist as well as non-Marxist circles. Studying these writings, spanning roughly four decades from 1889 till 1930, we may see dynamic interrelations between historical study, theory construction and contemporary political intervention. Kautsky's approach to such key Marxist concepts as class and state prove to be much more subtle and nuanced than what has commonly been assumed, incorporating the results of historical study rather than pure social theory. Yet, his account does contain important internal tensions and contradictions between agency and objective conditions and between the historical material and the normative and political perspective of the historiographer. Several of these internal tensions were carried on into mainstream Marxist accounts of the Revolution, with important historiographical as well as political consequences.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Nygaard examines how Kautsky deployed evolutionary and
  materialist categories in his historical interpretation of the French
  Revolution, arguing that Kautsky's method combined Darwinian stagism with
  Marxist class analysis. The article shows that Kautsky's "constructing"
  of Marxism involved not just theoretical synthesis but the application of
  evolutionary frameworks to concrete historical events, testing the limits
  of naturalistic explanation in political history.

  RELEVANCE: Illuminates how Kautsky's evolutionary naturalism operated at the
  level of historical practice -- how he actually applied his Darwinian-inflected
  historical materialism to interpret concrete history. Relevant to assessing
  the relationship between Kautsky's general evolutionary theory and his
  specific historical analyses.

  POSITION: Examines Kautsky as an active constructor of Marxist historiography
  through the application of evolutionary-materialist categories to historical
  events.
  },
  keywords = {Kautsky, French-Revolution, historiography, Marxism, evolutionary-materialism, Medium}
}

@incollection{weir2012riddles,
  author = {Weir, Todd H.},
  title = {The Riddles of Monism: An Introductory Essay},
  booktitle = {Monism},
  year = {2012},
  pages = {1--44},
  publisher = {Palgrave Macmillan US},
  doi = {10.1057/9781137011749_1},
  abstract = {Recent developments in the cognitive sciences point to a convergence, for some a clash, of the humanities and natural science. In 2004, a group of leading neurobiologists published a manifesto in the German journal Gehirn und Geist (Brain and Mind), claiming that advances in their discipline meant that it was poised to tackle the problems of consciousness and free will. In the growing field of evolutionary psychology, the leading research question "what is the evolutionary good of God?" indicates that religion has been subsumed under the explanatory framework of Darwinian natural selection. Against dualistic understandings of human reality, these varied scholarly endeavors seek to analyze nature and culture from a single vantage point. Philosophically, this stance is called monism.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Weir's introductory essay traces the history of Haeckelian
  monism as a philosophical-scientific worldview in Germany, arguing that
  monism served as the key intellectual framework linking Darwinian evolutionary
  biology to broader claims about social development, ethics, and politics.
  Weir shows that monism was politically diverse -- supporting both socialist
  and nationalist appropriations -- but that its core commitment to a unified
  naturalistic explanation of all phenomena made it a powerful vehicle for
  evolutionary social philosophy.

  RELEVANCE: Essential background for understanding the Haeckelian monist
  tradition that directly influenced Kautsky's naturalism. Kautsky's
  Vermehrung und Entwicklung and his ethics draw on monist assumptions about
  the continuity of natural and social evolution. Weir's essay provides the
  philosophical framework within which Kautsky's naturalism must be situated.

  POSITION: Analyzes monism as the philosophical mediator between Darwinian
  biology and social-political theory in Imperial Germany.
  },
  keywords = {Haeckel, monism, Darwinism, evolution, naturalism, Germany, socialist-ethics, High}
}

@article{stewart2019evolutionary,
  author = {Stewart, Ian G. and Hossfeld, Uwe and Levit, Georgy S.},
  title = {Evolutionary ethics and Haeckelian monism: the case of Heinrich Schmidt's Harmonie (1931)},
  journal = {Theory in Biosciences},
  year = {2019},
  volume = {138},
  number = {1},
  pages = {189--202},
  doi = {10.1007/s12064-019-00288-0},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: The paper examines the ethical philosophy of Heinrich Schmidt,
  Haeckel's student, as a case study in how Haeckelian monism generated a
  distinctive evolutionary ethics. Schmidt's system held that the moral good
  consists in what preserves and improves existence in accordance with
  evolutionary direction, with human social evolution following the same
  principle of increasing harmony that governs all natural evolution. The
  authors also examine Schmidt's failed attempt to adapt monist ethics to
  National Socialism, arguing that this failure shows the fundamental
  incompatibility of Haeckelian monism with fascism.

  RELEVANCE: Illuminates the specific philosophical framework -- evolutionary
  ethics grounded in monist naturalism -- that Kautsky drew upon in his Ethics
  and the Materialist Conception of History (1906). The Haeckelian tradition
  of deriving ethical norms from evolutionary naturalism is exactly the context
  in which Kautsky's own naturalistic ethics should be read.

  POSITION: Shows how Haeckelian monism produced an evolutionary ethics that
  could support socialist as well as other political conclusions, depending on
  which aspects of natural evolution were emphasized.
  },
  keywords = {Haeckel, monism, evolutionary-ethics, Schmidt, naturalism, socialism, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{stack2000firstdarwinian,
  author = {Stack, D. A.},
  title = {The First Darwinian Left: Radical and Socialist Responses to Darwin, 1859--1914},
  journal = {History of Political Thought},
  year = {2000},
  volume = {21},
  number = {4},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Stack surveys how radical and socialist thinkers from Alfred
  Russel Wallace to Ramsay MacDonald grappled with Darwinism in the period
  1859-1914. The article argues that socialist responses to Darwin were not
  simple adoption or rejection but involved selective appropriation of
  evolutionary concepts -- natural altruism, cooperative instincts,
  mutual aid -- that could support socialist rather than competitive-individualist
  conclusions. Stack distinguishes this "first Darwinian left" from the social
  Darwinist right by attending to which aspects of Darwin's theory each
  appropriated.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the comparative frame within which Kautsky's specific
  approach to Darwin can be assessed. By mapping the full range of
  socialist responses to Darwin in this period, Stack allows for a more
  precise characterization of what was distinctive about Kautsky's integration
  of evolutionary categories with Marxist social theory.

  POSITION: Argues for a specifically socialist or left-wing tradition of
  Darwinian appropriation distinct from and opposed to right-wing social
  Darwinism.
  },
  keywords = {Darwinism, socialism, Darwin-reception, left, evolution, socialist-Darwinism, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{rowley2024marxism,
  author = {Rowley, David G.},
  title = {Marxism as a Natural Science: Alexander Bogdanov's Anti-Revisionist Revisionism},
  journal = {Historical Materialism},
  year = {2024},
  volume = {32},
  number = {2},
  pages = {265--294},
  doi = {10.1163/1569206x-bja10035},
  abstract = {Discussion of Alexander Bogdanov as a Marxist revisionist has largely centred on his philosophy of being and cognition and on Plekhanov's and Lenin's accusation that Bogdanov was an idealist renegade from Marxism. However, the real issue of revisionism at the time was not materialism but determinism: the question of whether socialism would appear by the working of the objective laws of nature or the subjective will of human beings. Bogdanov did indeed revise Marxism, but he did so in order to defend it as a natural science. He conceived of society as a form of life subject to biological laws of nature: the need to assimilate energy from the environment and the need to adapt to the environment in the struggle to survive. He reinterpreted the key elements of scientific socialism -- the idea that existence determines consciousness and the labour theory of value -- in terms of this worldview.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Rowley argues that Alexander Bogdanov's project -- treating
  Marxism as a natural science on Darwinian and positivist grounds -- represented
  not a deviation from orthodoxy but an alternative within the same naturalistic
  paradigm that defined Second International Marxism. Bogdanov's empirio-monism
  radicalized the naturalistic premises that Kautsky and Engels shared, pushing
  toward a fully unified scientific worldview. The article traces how Bogdanov's
  version of "Marxism as natural science" competed with and ultimately lost to
  Leninist and Kautskyan orthodoxy.

  RELEVANCE: Provides a comparative perspective on Kautsky's naturalism by
  examining a contemporary who took the same naturalistic premises further.
  Useful for understanding what distinguished Kautsky's specific form of
  evolutionary naturalism from other available options in Second International
  Marxism, including Bogdanov's more radical version.

  POSITION: Situates Kautsky within a broader naturalistic-scientific paradigm
  in Second International Marxism, with Bogdanov as the more radical variant.
  },
  keywords = {Bogdanov, Kautsky, Marxism-natural-science, Second-International, naturalism, evolution, Medium}
}

@article{lattansi2018societal,
  author = {Lattansi-Silveus, Luke},
  title = {Societal Selection and Historical Materialism},
  journal = {Science \& Society},
  year = {2018},
  volume = {82},
  number = {3},
  pages = {335--359},
  doi = {10.1521/siso.2018.82.3.335},
  abstract = {Charles Darwin's The Origin of Species was welcomed by Marx and Engels, who saw it as similar to their methodology, but applied to the natural world. Following G. A. Cohen's analytical reconstruction of historical materialism, and the difficulties of the rationalist underpinnings Cohen gives it, some authors have viewed this interest in Darwin as indicative that Marx and Engels understood historical materialism to operate in a manner akin to natural selection. In particular, the work of Alan Carling, which proposes that market and military competition selects for greater development of the means of production, is very promising in this regard. But such a selective mechanism is not sufficient to under-pin historical materialism, as some criticisms show. An analysis of imitation is necessary to bolster the theory. Furthermore, the means by which superior productive power translate into competitive advantage need to be expounded.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: The paper examines whether Cohen's analytical reconstruction
  of historical materialism can be grounded in a mechanism analogous to natural
  selection -- specifically, whether market and military competition among
  societies "selects" for greater productive development. Building on Alan
  Carling's work, Lattansi-Silveus argues that while such a selectional
  mechanism is necessary to underpin historical materialism, it is insufficient
  without an account of imitation that explains how superior productive
  power translates into competitive advantage.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses the philosophical question of whether Darwinian
  selection logic can be coherently applied to social evolution -- the same
  question Kautsky faced in integrating Darwin with historical materialism.
  The paper provides an analytical reconstruction of the problem that illuminates
  what precisely was at stake in Kautsky's evolutionary reading of historical
  development.

  POSITION: Takes seriously the possibility of a selectionist mechanism
  grounding historical materialism while identifying the conditions it would
  need to satisfy.
  },
  keywords = {historical-materialism, natural-selection, social-evolution, Cohen, analytical-Marxism, Low}
}

@book{tai2019pannekoek,
  editor = {Tai, Chaokang and van der Steen, Bart and van Dongen, Jeroen},
  title = {Anton Pannekoek: Ways of Viewing Science and Society},
  year = {2019},
  publisher = {Amsterdam University Press},
  doi = {10.2307/j.ctvp7d57c},
  abstract = {Anton Pannekoek (1873-1960), prominent astronomer and world-renowned socialist theorist, stood at the nexus of the revolutions in politics, science and the arts of the early twentieth century. His astronomy was uniquely visual and highly innovative, while his politics were radical. Anton Pannekoek: Ways of Viewing Science and Society collects essays on Pannekoek and his contemporaries at the crossroads of political history, the history of science and art history.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: This edited volume examines Anton Pannekoek's unique synthesis
  of astronomy, scientific socialism, and council communism, arguing that his
  approach to natural science was integral to his political philosophy. Several
  chapters address Pannekoek's conception of Marxism as a natural science --
  his view that historical materialism had the same epistemological standing as
  Darwinian biology -- and trace how this shaped his break from Kautsky and the
  SPD mainstream over the role of natural-scientific determinism in socialist
  politics.

  RELEVANCE: Pannekoek was both a Kautsky disciple and a sharp critic of
  Kautsky's evolutionary determinism; his trajectory from Kautskyan naturalism
  to council communism illuminates what was philosophically at stake in the
  debates over evolution and Marxism in the Second International. The volume
  is useful for comparative contextualization of Kautsky's position.

  POSITION: Situates Pannekoek's scientific socialism as a distinct alternative
  to Kautsky's evolutionary orthodoxy within the same naturalistic paradigm.
  },
  keywords = {Pannekoek, scientific-socialism, natural-science, council-communism, Kautsky, evolution, Medium}
}

@article{leslie2020engels,
  author = {Leslie, Christopher},
  title = {Revisiting Friedrich Engels's Dialectics of Nature in an Age of Digital Idealism},
  journal = {tripleC: Communication, Capitalism \& Critique},
  year = {2020},
  volume = {19},
  number = {1},
  pages = {78--96},
  doi = {10.31269/triplec.v19i1.1223},
  abstract = {The idealism that Friedrich Engels seeks to defeat in Dialectics of Nature today pervades online discourse and pedagogies of science, technology, engineering, and mathematics (STEM). The deterministic view that STEM is dedicated to unleashing the inherent power in objects for the service of privileged societies fails to understand the basic principles that Engels proposed. Engels exposes his contemporaries' flawed understanding of science and technology and provides interdisciplinary examples that exemplify a different way of thinking. This analysis suggests avenues of cooperation for international science studies. In addition, it provides a starting point for pedagogies to promote the development for science and technology that reduces inequality and supports the notion that the liberal arts have an important place in the study of science and engineering.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Leslie revisits Engels's Dialectics of Nature as a text that
  sought to ground Marxist historical materialism in a comprehensive philosophy
  of natural science, arguing that Engels's project of extending dialectical
  materialism to nature was the foundational move that licensed later thinkers
  like Kautsky to treat social evolution as continuous with natural evolution.
  The paper examines the legacy of Engels's naturalism for subsequent Marxist
  engagements with science and technology.

  RELEVANCE: Engels's Anti-Duhring and Dialectics of Nature were the direct
  intellectual source for Kautsky's naturalistic reading of historical
  materialism. Understanding Kautsky's evolutionary framework requires grasping
  what he inherited from Engels's project of grounding Marxism in a unified
  philosophy of nature. This paper provides a critical perspective on that
  inheritance.

  POSITION: Reads Engels's naturalistic project as foundational for the
  Second International's evolutionary determinism, including Kautsky's.
  },
  keywords = {Engels, dialectics-of-nature, Marxism, naturalism, evolution, materialism, Low}
}
